       "24x24": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/45c30a3354c38331cc42d11962474554?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FJW-1.png",
       "32x32": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/45c30a3354c38331cc42d11962474554?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FJW-1.png",
       "48x48": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/45c30a3354c38331cc42d11962474554?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FJW-1.png"
      },
      "displayName": "Jared Whiklo",
      "self": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/user?accountId=6089a4c12a753d0071341edb",
      "timeZone": "America/Halifax"
     },
     "body": "<p>Did some work on this and I was able to use a cookie to track the transaction ID and send it with each AJAX request, but when you just view a resource you have a problem of it not being loaded by AJAX (and thus no Atomic-ID header is sent).</p>\n\n<p>The only solution I can think of it to make the entire HTML UI an app that loads the content from the back-end which would allow us to affect the sent headers. But that kinda defeats the simple HTML UI we were going for.</p>\n\n<p>Option B is to drop transaction support from the HTML UI.</p>",
     "created": "01/Jun/22 5:44 PM",
     "id": "22649",
     "jsdPublic": true,
     "self": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/issue/13732/comment/22649",
